# Git attributes for the Manvel 4X map mod.
#
# Why this file exists: GIANTS Editor, Farming Simulator itself, third-party GE
# scripts and our own Python tools all write these files with different line
# endings and BOM conventions. With no rules, git normalises them and then
# reports whole files as rewritten when the content is unchanged. Observed
# 2026-08-31: seeder1500NT.xml showed 649 insertions / 649 deletions, and both
# AutoDrive configs 130 each, all three byte-identical to HEAD once CR and BOM
# were normalised away. That churn buries real diffs and makes history useless
# for review.
#
# Disabling text conversion repo-wide means a file shows as modified only when
# its bytes genuinely differ. This suits a mod repo, where almost nothing is
# hand-authored in a normal editor and the tools that DO write these files are
# inconsistent and outside our control.
* -text

# Real binaries: never convert, never diff/merge as text. The five large,
# frequently-resaved asset types are routed through Git LFS instead of being
# stored inline, so history holds LFS pointers (~130 bytes each) rather than
# a new full binary blob on every GE resave.
*.dds    filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.png    filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.grle   filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.gdm    filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.shapes filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.zip    binary
*.pyc    binary

# GIANTS Editor terrain caches (*.i3d.terrain.{lod.type,nmap,occluders}.cache)
# are tracked and shipped, same as every GIANTS base map does. Routed through
# LFS: together they are ~71MB for this 4096 terrain and GE rewrites them on
# every save that touches the terrain. Without them the engine regenerates
# all three from the heightmap on every load, which took 10-15 minutes on an
# Apple M3 Pro (see CHANGELOG.md, "Load time investigation").
*.cache  filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# i3d files are XML, so they stay diffable, but they are machine-written by GE
# and can be enormous (map.i3d is ~4.6MB). Left as text deliberately: diffing
# them is how node/UserAttribute regressions get caught, which has mattered
# repeatedly on this project.
